docs/proposals/FOUNDATION.md:33:**Freeze:** mathematical scalar values, finite bit representations, operation contracts and error classification; representation and operation correspondence laws. Integer widths remain those named by `i8`…`i64` and `u8`…`u64`; proposed `usize` is separate. A bool has only false/true, encoded as byte 0/1. TYPE-20 makes char a one-byte code unit: every u8 round-trips through char. OD137 restricts character literals to ASCII; it does not restrict stored char values to ASCII.
docs/proposals/FOUNDATION.md:58:**Freeze:** blocks with fresh allocation identity, address space, base, extent, alignment and lifetime; byte initialization state; pointer provenance (the allocation a pointer originates from); a typed representation relation `Rep`; partial decoding, access validity and disjoint-memory composition laws. Pointer fragments carry provenance metadata separately from numeric bytes. Do not introduce a poison value that can become an arbitrary typed result.
docs/proposals/FOUNDATION.md:60:**State choice (question 2):** Core and its Lean evaluator use one hybrid state: structured places for safe typed storage, plus byte/provenance blocks for raw and exposed storage. A partial place-to-block/range projection and Rep relate the two. They are not independently writable copies. Safe borrow-view formation retains the structured root/projection and does not itself materialize bytes. Raw address-taking, expose, or passing an address to foreign code materializes the containing storage with a stable block/range mapping; existing materialized storage is reused. sizeof/alignof query layout without materializing storage. Materialization encodes active fields and pointer fragments and selects allowed initialization/byte contents for padding once through C8’s oracle.
docs/proposals/FOUNDATION.md:62:On a typed write to materialized storage, encode the new active value and update its bytes, initialization and provenance; preserve every byte, initialization bit and fragment outside the new active representation, including padding. Do not query fresh padding choices on this write. Newly active bytes must be initialized by the write; newly inactive bytes retain their previous contents. Lowerings must prove this store rule, including enum variant changes. An explicit raw copy follows its own byte-copy contract.
docs/proposals/FOUNDATION.md:64:Raw writes update bytes/fragments and invalidate overlapping cached typed values. Every safe typed read of a materialized place itself applies partial Rep decoding to the current bytes, initialization, provenance and permissions; no user-level decode instruction is needed. Successful decoding supplies the valid value. Failure is unsafe stuckness, never a fabricated value or implicit repair; safe-admission preservation must exclude it under the stated trusted premises. Unmaterialized typed reads use structured values. Raw pointers, expose/from_exposed and sizeof/alignof therefore already have Core meanings. Layout is a read-only parameter. Memory CFG makes this representation and ABI explicit for every operation; it does not introduce raw memory for the first time. This keeps typed proofs abstract while allowing slice 24 to execute without replacing Core state.
docs/proposals/FOUNDATION.md:66:**Rule:** memory bytes are initialized octets or uninitialized. Allocation starts uninitialized unless its primitive contract initializes it. Track initialization per occupied field/element; moving out removes that slot's typed ownership/read permission. Reading a typed value requires its active representation bytes initialized and valid. Padding and inactive enum storage need not be initialized and cannot affect typed equality, typed control or typed observations. Initialized padding may be observed through authorized raw byte reads and then affect output. Representation simulations preserve such observed bytes, or explicitly relate the same allowed padding choices; they cannot erase a raw observation as irrelevant nondeterminism. Raw byte copying may transport uninitialized padding and provenance fragments without observing them; observing a byte requires initialization. `Rep` ignores padding but checks active tags, bool/char/text validity and pointer fragments. Prove encoding/decoding round-trip for valid typed values, with allowed padding choices, and that decoding yields only valid values.
docs/proposals/FOUNDATION.md:68:Block identity is never reused, even if an address is. Deallocation ends all access rights to the old block. Nonzero accesses need provenance, bounds, liveness, alignment (or justified unaligned mode), initialization for reads and write permission for writes. Q3.7’s zero-byte raw access needs only alignment and a non-null address. One-past pointers may be formed but not dereferenced. Address arithmetic cannot silently wrap into another allocation. Integers carry no provenance; `expose` records the block in the address space, and `from_exposed` uses Q3.9's in-bounds-first, then one-past, then no-provenance rule over live, nonempty eligible exposed blocks. Do not add a new arbitrary tie-break where the decided relation permits choices. Pointer equality follows the decided raw model, not equality of allocation identities alone.
docs/proposals/FOUNDATION.md:78:**Rule:** loans retain the original root and projection. Keep **whole-root** conflicts, including disjoint fields: an exclusive loan conflicts with every other active access to that root; shared loans freeze the whole owner against mutation/move. A reborrow suspends conflicting access through its parent until the child ends. A loan-derived pointer is valid only during the loan. Shared views are Copy; mutable views are nonduplicable and end by borrow discipline, without an exactly-once destruction obligation. No optimization exploits exclusivity before the checker guarantee is proved.
docs/proposals/FOUNDATION.md:96:**Authority:** Core Q6.7, Q7.5–Q7.10, Q8.4–Q8.6; [runtime](../decisions.md#decided-project-runtime-2026-11-06), [stack bounds](../decisions.md#decided-2026-11-07-stack-randomness), [concurrency last](../decisions.md#decided-2026-11-07-final-slices). The sequential preservation obligation is an interface law, not a chosen concurrent model. **Later:** grant-specific events and adapters; concurrency and atomic laws at slice 28, last. Public environment effects are not deterministic merely because input replay is deterministic.
docs/proposals/FOUNDATION.md:104:Preserve wipe events as effects optimization cannot erase. Per allocator lineage report requested bytes, net change and peak logical bytes, plus allocator-defined footprint, each a sound bound with status or “not established”. Release/reset conserve accounting, and zero-size ownership adds no invented bytes. Bounds use polynomials with max over counts, lengths and depths; stack remains separate. This is the A2i instrumentation of R-0592, not later slice analysis.
docs/proposals/FOUNDATION.md:125:Prove erasure of ownership/brand/loan and accounting instrumentation and ghost computation: projected concrete executions and observations agree, and ghost state cannot alter runtime branches, authority or resources. Preserve diagnostic/evidence links even where code erases. Facts and diagnostics use one typed evidence graph and one versioned schema, with stable source sites; machine JSON views derive from it. A schema extension does not reinterpret old records.
docs/proposals/FOUNDATION.md:133:**Rule:** internal calls use an explicit target convention proved compatible with these contracts. At Memory CFG, lower the hybrid Core representation to explicit byte/provenance operations and ABI calls, proving its simulation against C4’s projection. BIR carries the complete resulting contract. Frame accounting binds to the final artifact, including spills, cleanup and launcher/runtime contributions, not merely source locals. Stack facts are separate from heap accounting. Foreign retention needs the decided `kept`/`Kept<H>` contract; FFI-safe types and no-enum restriction remain. Logical frame theorems do not add a source frame clause or snapshot construct.
docs/proposals/FOUNDATION.md:135:**Authority:** Core Q3.12, Q8.5–Q8.6, [pipeline](../decisions.md#decided-project-pipeline-2026-11-06), [contracts/P18](../decisions.md#decided-project-contracts), [stack bounds](../decisions.md#decided-2026-11-07-stack-randomness). **Later:** ABI implementations, extern syntax at 25, stronger program frame proofs. Neither the frame interface nor packed access waits until then.
docs/proposals/FOUNDATION.md:143:BIR feeds the LLVM printer or the proved BIR→LCB adapter. **Memory CFG stays the single layout/ABI/provenance lowering boundary**; retract P2's folding proposal. Earlier semantics uses C4’s hybrid state and read-only layout interface; representation simulations relate it to this explicit lowering boundary.
docs/proposals/FOUNDATION.md:147:Keep direct proofs for sequencing, cleanup, representation and the BIR→LCB adapter. Preserve the decided option of **proved validators for Mono and SSA**, as well as SSA optimizations, register allocation and linking. A validator's acceptance theorem establishes the required correspondence; running a checker without that theorem is not proof. Mono re-admits its output.
docs/proposals/FOUNDATION.md:163:| OD126; Q3.7/Q3.9/Q3.10 | Generalize address/profile interface, preserving provenance rules and zero-byte exception; existing 64-bit little-endian profile unchanged, including Q3.10’s `base + size ≤ 2^64−1` one-past bound. Any generalized profile needs an analogous checked bound. | 3 |
docs/proposals/FOUNDATION.md:167:| Q7.1/Q7.10; Q9.7 | Lawful registry instances rather than a permanently closed inventory; preserve existing grant contracts. Entropy details deferred. | 16, 14 |
docs/proposals/FOUNDATION.md:177:<a id="q1"></a>**Q1. Foundation scope.** Recommend approving the interface/law boundary of C1–C10, with freeze only after implementation, kernel proofs, interaction tests, independent review and owner acceptance. Runner-up: freeze smaller integrated scopes incrementally. Neither approves the other numbered questions.
docs/proposals/FOUNDATION.md:181:<a id="q5"></a>**Q5. Niches.** Three options: (a) exactly one null-empty/non-null single-pointer-payload niche, with pointer-size, provenance, ownership and release-context proofs; (b) tagged layout now, future niches only by owner decision; (c) no niches permanently. Recommend **(b)**: the foundation needs representation laws, not a permanent optimization inventory. Runner-up **(a)** gives compact optional ownership but adds a special eligibility proof; multiword release contexts and nullable raw pointers are ineligible. Round-2 reviewers split: Codex and Kimi prefer (b); agy prefers (c). (c) is uniform but unnecessarily permanent.
docs/proposals/FOUNDATION.md:188:<a id="q14"></a>**Q14. Entropy shape.** Recommend deferring exact launch-grant type, operations and ending contract to slice 6, consistent with Q7.10 and the randomness schedule. Runner-up: approve a nullary Entropy grant only together with a complete instance contract. No nullary shape is assumed by C5.
docs/proposals/FOUNDATION.md:190:<a id="q16"></a>**Q16. Registry extensibility.** Recommend lawful typed extensions preserving existing primitive/grant entries and exhaustive coverage of every selected instance; unknown operations are refused. Runner-up: retain closed Q9.7/Q7.10 inventories and seek an owner amendment for each addition. Schema compatibility must preserve old records; no new source spelling is implied.
