ROADMAP.md:441:| R-0493 | Typed arithmetic and casts from one semantics, with arithmetic-site facts | widths, signedness, modes and the defined trap match the Lean semantics; first applicable increment at B0; the approved slice-1 obligations remain due at B1 | R-0496 |
ROADMAP.md:443:| R-0501 | Proved lowering passes for sequencing, matching, cleanup and check expansion | each pass preserves behaviour and re-establishes invariants; the slice-0 instances are delivered at B0 and extended cumulatively at B1; every used lowering has preservation and output-invariant proofs | R-0514 |
ROADMAP.md:444:| R-0503 | The unverified BIR → LLVM path, the native driver and the pinned LLVM toolchain | output labelled unverified; LLVM, linker and runtime assumptions listed in their own inventory, separate from the LCB premises; the driver bounds external tools; the slice-0 instance is delivered at B0 and extended cumulatively at B1, labelled unverified | R-0501, R-0581 |
ROADMAP.md:445:| R-0581 | BIR as a complete backend contract shared by LLVM and the LCB ([decision](docs/decisions.md#decided-project-proof-infrastructure), 2026-11-06) | BIR's Lean definition fixes integer semantics, layout and endianness, alignment, zero-sized values, internal and foreign calling conventions, external events, trap realization (kind and site) and a frame-accounting handoff; no BIR construct, invariant or lowering choice depends on one backend; stack bytes are never derived from BIR slot counts, only from allocated frames in the final artifact; the slice-0 instance is delivered at B0 | R-0496, R-0502 |
ROADMAP.md:446:| R-0580 | A Memory CFG stage (layout and ABI lowering) between Place MIR and BIR | one layout authority (R-0502) assigns offsets, alignment, loads and stores over a block–offset memory model, consuming the spec's byte encoding; layout and provenance appear nowhere in SSA construction or either printer; the stage may be the identity in slice 0, with its preservation theorem stated, and is real by slice 9 (structs) | R-0502, R-0501 |
ROADMAP.md:447:| R-0583 | The per-pass proof-or-validator table | each pass is classified as proved directly (the stable, structural ones: parsing, sequencing, matching, cleanup, representation lowering, BIR → LCB-IR) or checked by a proved validator (the search-heavy or changing ones: specialization, SSA construction and destruction, optimizations, register allocation, scheduling, linking), with the reason; the lexer is proved against an independent byte/token judgment; an unclassified pass fails the gate; the slice-0 rows are delivered at B0 | R-0501, R-0504 |
ROADMAP.md:449:| R-0579 | The BIR → LCB-IR adapter refinement proof, the first backend proof target | the adapter's refinement against LCB's imported semantics (not a restatement) is kernel-checked for the slice-0 subset right after the front-end and lowering proofs, at B0, and extended to slice 1 at B1; the theorem is complete: an adapter error produces no artifact, and success implies correctness, so no adapter premise remains ([decision](docs/decisions.md#decided-2026-11-08-adapter)); the end-to-end theorem names the LLVM and LCB premises separately | R-0578, R-0501 |
ROADMAP.md:452:| R-0507 | Type and ownership soundness over the executable semantics | proved for slice 1; first applicable increment at B0; the approved slice-1 obligations remain due at B1 | R-0496, R-0499 |
ROADMAP.md:453:| R-0521 | Minimal provenance and one canonical verification task for the first authoritative claims | a claim names its compiler, source, theorem and executable digest; minimal provenance and kernel-checked per-compilation evidence accompany the first authoritative slice-0 claim; the evidence connects the exact input to the exact emitted BIR without trusting native evaluation, the backend input is bound to that BIR, and the Lean compiler, runtime and toolchain are listed as execution assumptions wherever the evidence does not close the gap (2026-11-06) | R-0509 |
ROADMAP.md:454:| R-0552 | Per-compilation certificates (owner-approved 2026-10-25) | a build emits a certificate per output, and the kernel checks it; minimal provenance and kernel-checked per-compilation evidence accompany the first authoritative slice-0 claim; each certificate connects the exact input to the exact emitted BIR through proved judgments or validators, checked by the kernel without trusting native evaluation; the backend input is bound to that BIR (a digest is identity, not correspondence); residual Lean compiler, runtime and toolchain trust is listed as explicit execution assumptions; LCB verification of compiled programs is not counted as verification of the compiler's execution (2026-11-06) | R-0504 |
ROADMAP.md:458:| R-0575 | The per-function facts report and query from slice 0 ([decision](docs/decisions.md#decided-project-facts-report), 2026-11-06) | every function of each slice has a report derived from the Lean rules, with a versioned schema: capabilities and the grants and resources actually reachable, allocation and allocator, trusted, foreign and opaque boundaries with their assumptions, each operation that could fail and how it is handled, termination (evidence with its named premises, or the responsible source permission and the call path to it), recursion depth and stack bound or why none is established, proof status per obligation, and completeness, each with its reason, evidence and source location; a permission that justifies nothing is reported as a fact, which a `Concrete.toml` policy may refuse (the decided refusals of an unmatched `#[proof]` and a duplicate `#[may_not_terminate]` stay) (carried from slice 0 into CheckedCore and the schema, spec obligations O1 and O4); the schema already has fields for what memory a function writes (frames) and what its outputs depend on (dependency flow), reported "not established" until derived: definite, maybe and never writes per `&mut` place and the output-to-input dependencies (control dependence, cleanup and opaque calls included) are derived into these fields as soon as `&mut` lands, not in E–G, while R-0524, R-0473 and R-0383 keep source frames, contracts and package summaries; facts are preserved across indirect calls, specialization and package boundaries (R-0588); from slice 0 the compiler answers a query about one function or module from the same facts, as readable text and in the machine format (the command is `concrete facts`); the report never claims what the rules do not establish; every fact is a typed judgment in Lean with a derivation-soundness theorem (and obligation-discovery completeness where it claims absence), one derivation feeds the readable view, the machine format and every policy, each fact has a closed status (established, conditional, refuted, not established, not applicable) with per-fact coverage, unknown never satisfies a policy, and answers carry typed identities, versioned schema and semantics and artifact digests; from slice 0 the report also carries the build identity (compiler SHA, toolchain pins and target), each program's maximum stack use, measured from the final binary on both backends, and the trusted-base statement naming the Lean kernel, LLVM printer and LLVM, LCB's MIR→assembly step, linker, OS and named runtime premises as not proven ([early decisions](docs/decisions.md#decided-2026-11-07-early-evidence)); the diagnostics and facts JSON schema is frozen and versioned from the first front-end output, with golden tests; a planted wrong fact and a planted unknown version are caught; Home: spec (the stack-bound claim and, from slice 0, the refusal of an unknown or over-budget bound against the `Concrete.toml` stack budget) and tooling (the report). Gate: stack, the final-artifact bound checked against the claim and the budget ([tracking](docs/decisions.md#decided-2026-11-07-tracking)) | R-0499 |
ROADMAP.md:459:| R-0622 | The written grammar before the parser ([decision](docs/decisions.md#decided-2026-11-08-written-grammar)) | the full surface grammar is written down for the owner's review, every construct with its decided row and the open spellings flagged; the owner approves it; it is encoded in Lean as the independent grammar judgment (row Q8.3, SPEC_UNIFICATION step 8's `Grammar`); only then is the parser built against it; this applies before slice 0's parser and blocks B0's R-0620 completion; Home: spec (`Grammar`), after a review sheet outside the repository. Gate: the owner's recorded approval, then the grammar's conformance examples and `ll1_sound` | owner: the grammar review |
ROADMAP.md:460:| R-0620 | The lexer and the LL(1) parser, started now in parallel with the foundation ([decision](docs/decisions.md#decided-2026-11-08-front-end)) | the lexer for slice 0's surface runs against the spec's `Lexical` rules as they land (SPEC_UNIFICATION step 8) and does not wait for R-0622, which only the parser and so this task's completion wait for; and the parser is built against the Lean grammar judgment once the owner has approved the written grammar (R-0622), with refusals as diagnostics records (R-0593, which this task feeds rather than waits for); correspondence to the grammar is proved as part of B0; Home: tooling (the compiler's front end), against the spec. Gate: the conformance examples of slice 0's lexical and grammar rules, accepted and refused | R-0622 |
ROADMAP.md:461:| R-0593 | The diagnostic and facts records, their frozen, versioned JSON schema, goldens and the parser's diagnostics from the first front-end output ([diagnostics decision](docs/decisions.md#decided-project-diagnostics-2026-11-06), [early decision](docs/decisions.md#decided-2026-11-07-early-evidence)) | every slice-0 refusal is one typed record rendered as human text and JSON lines (`--format=human|json`), with rule IDs from the Lean rule name and that rule's own revision (R-0603); the diagnostics and facts JSON schema is frozen and versioned from the first front-end output, and golden tests cover it and both diagnostic renderings; the LL(1) parser fills its expected-token set from the grammar data; B0's classified refusal controls use the records; Home: spec (the diagnostics and facts types are the schema source). Gate: the JSON schema generated from the Lean types, with goldens ([tracking](docs/decisions.md#decided-2026-11-07-tracking)) | R-0592, R-0575 |
ROADMAP.md:462:| R-0594 | Zero-heap facts proved in slice 0 ([decision](docs/decisions.md#decided-project-memory-2026-11-06)) | every slice-0 function's heap facts are 0, established by the derivation (no allocator is reachable), not by schema defaults; a planted reachable allocator flips them | R-0575, R-0592 |
ROADMAP.md:463:| R-0585 | Vacuity checks and contract-strength labels from the first proof slice | no fact reads "proved" when its theorem holds only under an unsatisfiable precondition or premise set, from the first proof slice on; the checker reports a context as infeasible wherever its octagon is empty, and a discharge inside an infeasible context is marked vacuous, never established, unless the infeasibility itself is the claim (a dead branch), applying OD120 from B1 rather than F; each proved claim carries its strength (a point instance, one direction, an equivalence or a full contract), so a single-input proof never reads as a universal contract; planted vacuous and point-only proofs are caught; obligation sufficiency stays with R-0460 and the phase-F validation project with R-0219 | R-0575 |
ROADMAP.md:464:| R-0599 | Replayed counterexamples for "not established", without SMT | a deterministic bounded search evaluates a failed obligation's path constraints on boundary points (type extremes and the octagon's constants) and replays a candidate through the Lean reference evaluator with the exact semantics, inputs, resources and environment, satisfying the preconditions; a replayed witness reports "refuted, replayed", otherwise "no witness within N cases", never "safe"; the search never affects admission; due at B1 | R-0575, R-0586 |
ROADMAP.md:467:| R-0586 | The I/O driver for the Lean reference evaluator | the Lean evaluator runs programs that read and write their grants, so I/O examples get expected outcomes independent of the compiler; its infrastructure lands with slice 1 (B1), and from slice 16 (the console) it runs every supported I/O example and produces its expected observations independently of the compiler, and R-0491 uses them | R-0496, R-0425 |
ROADMAP.md:471:| R-0118 | Generated coverage of passes, consumers, constructs, operations and traps | a missing row or a synthetic constructor fails the gate; B0 includes pipeline-edge coverage and controls for missing proofs, unregistered premises, wrong artifact substitution and reject-all compilation | R-0501 |
ROADMAP.md:473:| R-0498 | Every enforcement claim maps to its gate leg and a targeted mutation | an unmapped claim fails; shape checks are labelled unproved; B0 includes pipeline-edge coverage and controls for missing proofs, unregistered premises, wrong artifact substitution and reject-all compilation | R-0118 |
ROADMAP.md:475:| R-0550 | Compiler speed, scaling and binary-size tracking from slice 0 ([speed decision](docs/decisions.md#decided-2026-11-07-performance), [early decision](docs/decisions.md#decided-2026-11-07-early-evidence)) | wall time, per-stage time and memory are recorded by exact SHA, backend and target from slice 0; binary size is tracked per example per commit next to speed tracking and is report-only; a compile-speed budget is tracked per example, report-only like binary size ([decision](docs/decisions.md#decided-2026-11-08-compile-budget)); speed measurements are report-only until their noise is understood, then scaling gates hold for each stage and a planted regression is caught; Home: tooling. Gate: speed and binary size, report-only ([tracking](docs/decisions.md#decided-2026-11-07-tracking)) | R-0503 |
ROADMAP.md:476:| R-0553 | Totality audit: every semantics, checker, validator and pass is total and runs as proved | no partial definition; opaque execution gaps controlled; totality and execution-gap checks apply from B0; B3 performs the cumulative audit after B2 | R-0501 |
ROADMAP.md:479:| R-0569 | A new website, built from scratch with the new compiler (decision row P9) | the site is designed anew around the decided language; its playground runs the new compiler; it teaches and links the Lean rules and states no rule of its own; no page describes the legacy compiler or the language before the 2026-11 decisions as current | B1 |
ROADMAP.md:485:| R-0572 | The LCB pilot (LP0): a BIR → LCB → native proof on the subset of the slices before LP0, before substantial feature growth (decided 2026-11-06, revised the same day: the LCB joins slice 0) | the subset, already built unverified through the LCB since slice 0 (R-0578), carries a native proof: the adapter proof (R-0579) composed with the LCB's theorems for its exact domain and with the slice theorem under named premises, kept separate from the LLVM premises; every remaining backend obligation has its own task and milestone; native proof closure for the profile (R-0517) follows | B1, R-0579 |
ROADMAP.md:495:| R-0587 | Debug info on the LLVM path | unverified DWARF output on the LLVM profile, with a check that enabling it changes no generated code | R-0503 |
ROADMAP.md:510:| R-0137 | Workload validation: each workload records its attempts, diagnostics and measurements | the dashboard covers every supported workload; workloads grow from simple proved examples to file-copy and then supported real programs, each recording program correctness, authority, failure behaviour and residual assumptions; every port ships paired rejected-violation examples showing what Concrete refuses | C |
ROADMAP.md:535:| R-0460 | Obligation sufficiency, with hypothesis provenance | each admitted obligation is proved to suffice | R-0495 |
ROADMAP.md:537:| R-0482 | Separate identities for model, implementation, proposition and contract | the relation and the substitution matrix are checked | R-0473 |
ROADMAP.md:542:| R-0259 | Runtime-safety obligations: casts, loops, stack, panics, byte, text and path boundaries | the safety corpus is discharged or reported | R-0495 |
ROADMAP.md:547:| R-0424 | Performance evidence: flagship runtime tracking from slice 2, then corpus, codegen quality, fair comparisons and optimization ownership ([early tracking decision](docs/decisions.md#decided-2026-11-07-performance)) | flagship runtime is recorded by exact SHA, backend and target from slice 2 and is report-only until its noise is understood; the mixed corpus runs on LLVM and LCB-native; Home: tooling. Gate: flagship speed, report-only ([tracking](docs/decisions.md#decided-2026-11-07-tracking)) | R-0517 |
ROADMAP.md:556:| R-0512 | Claims on compiler-generated Core statements; no authority from legacy evidence | identity, binding and provenance are rebuilt on new Core | R-0494 |
ROADMAP.md:557:| R-0178 | Stable machine-readable fact schemas and compatibility policy for the frozen, versioned diagnostics and facts JSON schema introduced from the first front-end output ([early decision](docs/decisions.md#decided-2026-11-07-early-evidence)) | the schemas remain versioned and frozen, and the compatibility policy holds; Home: spec (the schema source) and tooling (the compatibility check). Gate: the generated JSON schema, with goldens ([tracking](docs/decisions.md#decided-2026-11-07-tracking)) | R-0494 |
ROADMAP.md:559:| R-0201 | Audit commands over the slice-0 facts query (R-0575): `audit`, `explain`, `why`, `diff` and trust-diff gates | the package-scale audit bundle passes; the per-function query itself is R-0575 (B0/B1), not this task | R-0178 |
