docs/decisions.md:1292:| OD2 | <a id="od2"></a>`&mut` arguments: an implicit reborrow; overlapping exclusive arguments refused; a deferred call keeps its loan until it runs; one defined order for cleanup, loan end and write-back | D1 and §4.5; the reborrow implemented in landing train 6 (REF-10); the defer and exit-order rules not yet | exit 2i (G9, B1, B2), so every deliverable; R-0496 (Core reborrow form) | decided 2026-10-29 by the owner: (1) passing a `&mut` on is plain lending, each call's use ending when it returns, with no reborrow concept or syntax; (2) a call is refused when an exclusive argument overlaps any other argument (`h(r, r)`, `h(&mut x, &x)`, `h(&mut a, &mut a.f)` refused); as tightened by OD15 (a) the same day, two arguments that borrow the same root conflict whenever one is exclusive, so even `h(&mut a.f, &mut a.g)` is refused; (3) `&mut` refers to the place, a write through it is the write to that place at once, and copy-in/copy-out is not part of the language (only an optimization proven equivalent); (4) `defer` keeps OWN-12 and a borrow passed to a deferred call stays live until the call runs; (5) one exit sequence on every non-abort exit: the exit value, then the scope's deferred calls (last registered first, innermost scope first), then the scope's loans end, then the remaining locals are destroyed; an abort runs none of it **Canonical current root-based exclusivity rule: REF-11 and OD15(a); this historical overlap formulation is a reference to that rule.** |
docs/decisions.md:1298:| OD8 | <a id="od8"></a>Global state: immutable globals everywhere; mutable state only as declared cells in unsafe-marked modules, reached through capabilities; no `static mut` | D8; pending (TRUST-6, R-0509) | R-0509, R-0496; V6 | decided 2026-10-29 by the owner: Austral's capability model, extended. (1) Constants only: no mutable global state of any kind (no `static mut`, no cells); a constant is truly immutable, has no interior mutability and never contains a capability, token or handle. (2) Global resources are linear tokens minted at startup: `main` receives a root resource value (in the spirit of Austral's `RootCapability`) and splits it; hardware and runtime singletons (allocator arena, UART and MMIO, panic buffer, errno, C libraries with internal state) are produced or taken only by `trusted fn` wrappers returning or taking linear tokens, and a gate lists those wrappers. (3) Tokens are ordinary values: every effect shows as a token parameter, under the existing loan and exclusivity rules (OD1, OD2, OD15), with no separate effect system. (4) Interrupt handlers, FFI callbacks and future threads get only the tokens explicitly given to them, by installation, transfer or a lock granting a borrow. (5) No life before `main`, no cleanup protocol for global state, and abort runs nothing (OD10). (6) An implicit-context mechanism, if ergonomics later demands one, is a new owner decision, never a hidden global. The cell variant was rejected after the Codex and Grok reviews (interrupts, FFI re-entrancy, linear cell contents, effect polymorphism, concurrency). Now TRUST-8 to TRUST-10 (pending, R-0557) Revised 2026-11-01 – 11-02: items 2 and 3 are superseded by the authority decision (grants handed to `main`; `with(...)` plus resource values); item 5 stands ([decisions](decisions.md#decided-2026-11-02-authority)) |
docs/decisions.md:1305:| OD15 | <a id="od15"></a>G10: whole-owner freezing under a shared borrow stays. G19: no pass assumes `&mut` exclusivity, and the operational loan rules are defined now | T-G10 (keep the freeze) and T-G19 (the policy sentence) | exit 2i (the G10 decision record), so every deliverable; R-0496, R-0505 | decided 2026-10-29 by the owner. (a) Whole-owner freezing: a live borrow of any part of a value freezes the whole owner, and loans are keyed by the root binding (REF-11 as in the current compiler); this tightens OD2's part 2: at a call, two arguments that borrow the same root conflict whenever one is exclusive, even disjoint fields (`h(&mut a.f, &mut a.g)` is refused, as in the current compiler); OD1's "keyed by the root variable" stands. (b) The strict option: the language guarantees `&mut` exclusivity, which the checker enforces (OD2, OD15a), and no compiler pass or optimization may assume or exploit it until the checker's guarantee is proven in Lean (R-0499 with the Core semantics R-0496); until then the operational loan rules are defined now, G19 included: a pointer or alias derived from a loan is valid only while that loan lives. Now REF-11@3 (partial, bug 319, which OD23 adds) and REF-14 (pending, R-0496) **Canonical current root-based exclusivity subject: REF-11 (including OD23’s re-lent case); OD2 and OD23 refer here rather than defining separate variants.** |
docs/decisions.md:1327:| OD37 | <a id="od37"></a>The names of the pointer difference and ordering functions (added 2026-10-29, while recording OD26). Options: `ptr::diff(p, q) -> Int` and `ptr::cmp(p, q) -> Ordering` beside `ptr::offset`; or no ordering at all, only `ptr::addr(p)` (via `ptr::expose`) compared as integers. Recommendation: `ptr::diff` and `ptr::cmp`, both trusted, each with its provenance premise (same allocation) stated as an obligation; Austral has no named pointer-difference or ordering functions, and its `Pointer` and `Address` types are compared with the ordering operators Reviewed pick (2026-10-30, a recommendation that supersedes the recommendation above where they differ): one trusted `ptr::diff` (same-allocation premise), ordering read from its sign, no `ptr::cmp`; specify the representability of its result, one-past pointers included (Q8 is marked agreed in the picks; ROADMAP records no owner answer for it) | trusted code compares raw pointers with `==` and `<` today (bug 322) | bug 322's repair (TYPE-6, TRUST-1) | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): One trusted `ptr::diff`, ordering from its sign, no `ptr::cmp` |
docs/decisions.md:1331:| OD41 | <a id="od41"></a>PAT-9 and integer ranges that span their type (added 2026-10-29, while fixing bug 317). Options: a catch-all after ranges covering the whole integer type is not refused (PAT-2 still requires it); or it is refused and PAT-2 is relaxed to accept ranges that cover the type. Recommendation: not refused, keeping PAT-2 as decided Reviewed pick (2026-10-30, a recommendation that supersedes the recommendation above where they differ): close as range-specific under OD56, recording in PAT-2 and PAT-9 that an integer match still needs a catch-all and that listing every literal is not coverage | such a match is accepted | bug 317's repair (PAT-9, PAT-2) | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): Close as range-specific; integer matches keep a required catch-all, listing every literal is not coverage |
docs/decisions.md:1354:| OD64 | <a id="od64"></a>Raw pointers derived from a borrow (added 2026-10-30, item G19 of the owner picks of that day). Question: What a raw pointer derived from a borrow may do: whether it is valid only while the borrow lives, may escape the borrow block, may be passed to C, and how extern parameters say so. Options: (a) valid only while the borrow lives and never escaping it; (b) the operational loan rules of REF-14 only. Pick (the coordinator's pick after the Kimi, Codex and Grok review of 2026-10-30, a recommendation and not a decision): (a): no returning, no escaping storage (a local `let` inside the block stays allowed), no converting to an integer and no capturing in an outer `defer`; borrow-derived pointers go only to call-only `extern` parameters; C uses it only during a call (memory C keeps must come from an allocation with its own owner); `extern` parameters say call-only (the default) or kept; field pointers come from the same borrow of the whole owner; deferred calls in the block run before the borrow ends; provenance survives copies, offsets, field pointers, helpers and indirect calls; a shared borrow never yields write access; `ptr::expose` is refused on such a pointer; the annotation's syntax gets an LL(1) check. Austral: not checked | no pass assumes `&mut` exclusivity (OD15); REF-14 bounds aliases by their loan | REF-14, TRUST-1, TRUST-3, FFI-3, OWN-12 | decided 2026-10-31 by the owner's rule (every reviewer agreed, as strict as Austral or stricter): Valid only inside the borrow; call-only extern params |
docs/decisions.md:1405:| OD115 | <a id="od115"></a>The body and totality of a specification function (added 2026-10-30, raised while absorbing the verification documents). Question: Whether a `spec fn` may be declared without a body, and whether its body must be shown total. Bears on CON-3, CON-5, CON-1. The current compiler: both `spec fn big(x: Int) -> bool;` and `spec fn big(x: Int) -> bool = x > 10;` are accepted, and so is a recursive body that never terminates (`spec fn loopy(x: Int) -> bool = loopy(x + 1);`), with no check. [CONTRACTS_GUIDE.md](../legacy/docs/verification/CONTRACTS_GUIDE.md) calls a `spec fn` "an erased mathematical specification, no body"; contracts.md's examples (CON-3) give it a body; [CONTRACTS_AND_VCS.md](../legacy/docs/verification/CONTRACTS_AND_VCS.md) (Soundness Bridge) requires it to be total. Options: (a) a body is required and must be total (structural recursion or a checked measure); (b) a body is optional, a body-less one an uninterpreted function a proof must constrain, and a body must be total; (c) as in the current compiler, no totality check. Austral: not applicable. Pick: none given (raised by the absorption of docs/verification, 2026-10-30). | see OPEN-115 | see OPEN-115 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): Body required and total |
docs/decisions.md:1716:- <a id="OPEN-41"></a>**OPEN-41** (OD41, PAT-9 and integer ranges that span their type): See [OD41](#od41).
docs/decisions.md:1753:- <a id="OPEN-115"></a>**OPEN-115** (OD115, The body and totality of a specification function): See [OD115](#od115).

