rg: legacy/docs/spec/secrets.md: No such file or directory (os error 2)
legacy/docs/spec/ffi.md:6:declarations (FFI-3), layout attributes (FFI-4), symbol names (FFI-5), compiler intrinsics (FFI-6) and
legacy/docs/spec/ffi.md:7:what layout is promised (FFI-7 to FFI-12). The design record is
legacy/docs/spec/ffi.md:68:<a id="FFI-3"></a>
legacy/docs/spec/ffi.md:69:### FFI-3. An extern declaration declares its effects and FFI-safe types
legacy/docs/spec/ffi.md:71:**FFI-3@1.** An `extern fn` has no body, declares its effects with `with(...)` (`with()` for none, never for a C symbol known to have effects), and takes and returns only FFI-safe types: integers, floats, `bool`, `char`, `Unit`, raw pointers to a type that contains no reference, and `#[repr(C)]` structs.
legacy/docs/spec/ffi.md:201:<a id="FFI-5"></a>
legacy/docs/spec/ffi.md:202:### FFI-5. Concrete symbols cannot collide with C symbols
legacy/docs/spec/ffi.md:204:**FFI-5@1.** Every Concrete-defined function is emitted under a mangled symbol in a namespace disjoint from C identifiers, and only an `extern fn` (which binds its `#[link_name]` or its declared name) and an `#[export_name]` function use a C name.
legacy/docs/spec/ffi.md:258:import foo.{sizeof};
legacy/docs/spec/ffi.md:259:fn main() -> u8 { if sizeof::<u8>() + sizeof::<u32>() == 5 { return 42; } return 0; }
legacy/docs/spec/ffi.md:261:#[intrinsic = "sizeof"]
legacy/docs/spec/ffi.md:262:pub trusted fn sizeof<T>() -> u64;
legacy/docs/spec/ffi.md:266:fn main() -> u8 { if sizeof::<u8>() == 1 { return 42; } return 0; }
legacy/docs/spec/ffi.md:269:<a id="FFI-7"></a>
legacy/docs/spec/ffi.md:270:### FFI-7. Layout is unspecified unless a `repr` fixes it
legacy/docs/spec/ffi.md:272:**FFI-7@1.** The field order, offsets, padding, size and alignment of a struct or enum are unspecified except as a struct's `#[repr]` attribute fixes them (FFI-8), and safe code observes them only through `sizeof::<T>()` and `alignof::<T>()`, which give `T`'s size and alignment in bytes as a `u64`.
legacy/docs/spec/ffi.md:287:code that walks a buffer may also rely on the stride of an array of `T` being `sizeof::<T>()`
legacy/docs/spec/ffi.md:290:```con accept name=observed-through-sizeof exit=42
legacy/docs/spec/ffi.md:297:    if sizeof::<S>() >= 5 && alignof::<S>() >= 1 {
legacy/docs/spec/ffi.md:318:**Rationale.** `repr(C)` is what a struct needs to cross `extern fn` (FFI-3), and `repr(packed)`
legacy/docs/spec/ffi.md:334:    if sizeof::<R>() == 8 && alignof::<R>() == 4 && sizeof::<H>() == 7 && alignof::<H>() == 1
legacy/docs/spec/ffi.md:335:        && sizeof::<P>() == 5 && alignof::<P>() == 1 {
legacy/docs/spec/ffi.md:370:owns, stays a plain `extern fn`. The effects half is FFI-3: every `extern fn` declares its effects.
legacy/docs/spec/ffi.md:397:**FFI-10@1.** The only layout attributes are `#[repr(C)]`, `#[repr(packed)]` and `#[repr(align(N))]`, alone or combined as FFI-4 allows and written only on a struct, so every other struct has the unspecified layout of FFI-7, and no other `repr` option, `transparent` included, and no `repr` on an enum is accepted.
legacy/docs/spec/ffi.md:503:### FFI-12. The stride of a buffer is `sizeof`
legacy/docs/spec/ffi.md:505:**FFI-12@2.** Consecutive elements of an array, a `HeapArray` or a raw buffer of `T` lie `sizeof::<T>()` bytes apart, which trusted code doing pointer arithmetic over the elements may rely on.
legacy/docs/spec/ffi.md:518: This is the one layout fact beyond FFI-7 and FFI-8 that trusted code needs to walk a
legacy/docs/spec/ffi.md:520:`sizeof::<T>()`. The examples use the pointer arithmetic of the current compiler (`p + 1` and an
