552:R-0553	Totality audit: every semantics, checker, validator and pass is total and runs as proved	retained	B		Require new semantics, checkers, validators and proved passes to be total and executed as proved, with an environment inventory and controls for opaque or replacement execution gaps.		Require new semantics, checkers, validators and proved passes to be total and executed as proved, with an environment inventory and controls for opaque or replacement execution …	
221:- Its root is `t`'s root plus a byte-range projection (Q3.3).
292:## F6. The hosted target table (Q7.1–Q7.8) — high
294:> **Superseded in part 2026-11-05 (owner, row Q7.11, [decisions.md](../../decisions.md#decided-2026-11-05-revisions)):** the standard streams use the new `Console` capability, not `Device` (OPEN F6.1 is answered with option (c), revising authority rule 13). Every `with(Device)` on a `StdIn`, `StdOut` or `StdErr` operation in the table below reads `with(Console)`. The text below is kept as history.
306:`main` declares the grants it wants as parameters. The launcher mints exactly those and maps each parameter name to the launch configuration. A parameter type this target does not offer is a compile error. A configured name that is missing refuses to start, before `main`, with exit code 125 *(value: owner)*.
308:| grant type | instances | launch config | operations (rights) | narrowing / descendants | ending it |
312:| `File` (acquired, not granted) | — | — | per open mode | none | `close(f) with(Fs) -> Result<Unit, CloseError>`; consumes in every outcome (Linux `close` always releases the descriptor); `CloseError` is Copy and holds no handle |
314:| `Clock<Monotonic>`, `Clock<Wall>` | ≤ 1 each | — | `now(&c) with(Time) -> u64` ns; resolution from `clock_getres`, recorded in the launch manifest; Wall may go backward | none | `give_up(c) with(Time) -> Unit` |
315:| `StdIn`, `StdOut`, `StdErr` | ≤ 1 each | — | read or write bytes, `with(Device)` (see OPEN F6.1) | none | `give_up(s) with(Device) -> Result<Unit, IoError>`; flushes; consumes in every outcome; the descriptor stays open for the runtime's trap message |
323:- `Start(grants)`: the launcher mints the requested grants.
325:- `Trap(kind)`: the runtime writes a fixed message to descriptor 2 and exits with status 134.
338:**OPEN F6.1 — owner: the permission for the standard streams.** The reserved set (`decisions.md:410`) has no `Console`. The options:
495:Generic bodies are checked parametrically, then rechecked after substitution (Q6.3). Unreachable branches are checked (Q6.4).
506:- "Check all written code" (Q6.4) makes an initializer with a proven violation refused **even when the constant is unused**.
528:> The first milestone proves the compiler's front end and lowering correct from exact source to BIR, for slice 1, in Lean. The native build is qualified by differential testing, not proved: the printer, LLVM, linking and runtime are named assumptions. A claim about the native image is conditional on those assumptions. The approved launcher and target manifest is `linux-x86_64-hosted` (F6).
538:- **P12, revised.** From day one there is a minimal `ISSUES.tsv` at the root, with the columns `id, date, area (spec|proof|compiler|launcher|ci|migration), evidence (path or SHA), owner, severity, closure condition, status`. A small root check validates the columns and statuses. The full bug ledger and tool return with slice 1.
581:- **F6** — the target table and the exit code 125.
582:- **F6.1** — the stream permission. `Device` is recommended; `Console` would revise rule 13.
docs/proposals/FOUNDATION.md:3:Status: the owner answered "ok all recommended" on 2026-11-08 to the proposal's sixteen questions ([record](../decisions.md#decided-2026-11-08-foundation)), after choosing foundation-first on 2026-11-07 with "ok to option b then for freeze the foundation>?" ([record](../decisions.md#decided-2026-11-07-foundation-first)). The plan for PLAN steps 3+; the Lean spec stays the only home of rules.
docs/proposals/FOUNDATION.md:5:The [A2i decision](../decisions.md#decided-project-long-term-2026-11-06) freezes integrated scopes, "not an untested whole-language architecture". Here the frozen foundation means interfaces and laws implemented, proved and tested together, with representative interactions. It does not mean designing every future slice before slice 1. Later slices supply lawful instances; changes to decided rules return to the owner.
docs/proposals/FOUNDATION.md:7:References below use the actual anchors in this checkout. **Core rows** means [Q1–Q9](../decisions.md#decided-2026-11-05-core). The pipeline referred to in the brief as the 2026-11-07 decision is recorded here under [the 2026-11-06 pipeline anchor](../decisions.md#decided-project-pipeline-2026-11-06); there is no `decided-2026-11-07-pipeline` anchor. The [authoritative core-row notes](../archive/decisions-2026-11-05/core_rows.json) supplement these rows. Proposed additions without a decided row are identified as such.
docs/proposals/FOUNDATION.md:15:Before freezing, implement these contracts in the authoritative Lean library, with readable comments, accepted/refused examples and counterexamples to invalid premises. Discharge the four Q9.14 design theorems and the [A2i interaction/erasure obligations](../decisions.md#decided-project-long-term-2026-11-06), including the laws below. Tests alone do not establish them. Existing `ConcreteSpec` fragments are starting material, not evidence that omitted pointer fragments, external frames, initialization or cleanup interactions are already covered. Review and owner approval precede the freeze. This is a writing proposal, not that completed review or approval.
docs/proposals/FOUNDATION.md:19:These are contracts to complete, not declarations that the proofs exist. Decided rules remain binding. New semantic completions and revisions are separately answerable in section 5; none is approved by this draft.
docs/proposals/FOUNDATION.md:27:Keep the decided Core loop condition region, counted-loop bound snapshots and counter advancement. Preserve the restrictions on exits inside operands. Return evaluates its result before leaving scopes. Normal block exit, return, break and continue run the deferred calls of each exited scope, inner scope first and reverse registration order within a scope. Deferred operands are read at cleanup, with dependencies kept alive. If cleanup aborts, stop immediately; no remaining cleanup runs. Explicit abort runs no deferred call and does not unwind. Ordinary locals do not acquire implicit user destructors: explicit consumption, storage teardown and resource destruction remain different operations. Q2.3 fixes sequence destruction in increasing index order, then backing; OrderedMap in ascending key order, key before value, then nodes; Heap payload before cell; Option/Result destroy the present payload. User types follow their checked destroy bodies. Q3.8 explicitly preserves index order for zero-sized elements. Destruction is an ordinary checked call and may itself fail to terminate unless stronger evidence exists.
docs/proposals/FOUNDATION.md:33:**Freeze:** mathematical scalar values, finite bit representations, operation contracts and error classification; representation and operation correspondence laws. Integer widths remain those named by `i8`…`i64` and `u8`…`u64`; proposed `usize` is separate. A bool has only false/true, encoded as byte 0/1. TYPE-20 makes char a one-byte code unit: every u8 round-trips through char. OD137 restricts character literals to ASCII; it does not restrict stored char values to ASCII.
docs/proposals/FOUNDATION.md:35:**Rule:** signed representation is two's complement. Division truncates toward zero; nonzero remainder has the dividend's sign. Zero divisors and signed `MIN / -1` and `MIN % -1` are errors, the latter two `Overflow`. Shift counts satisfy `0 ≤ count < width`; valid shifts discard outgoing bits, signed right shift sign-fills and unsigned right shift zero-fills. Plain arithmetic needs closed safety evidence. Checked operations return the decided `Result<T, ArithmeticError>`; wrapping operations compute modulo the width and saturating operations clamp to bounds. `as` is lossless over the entire source domain; lossy conversions use named operations. OD106 and ARITH-10 fix `try_convert -> Result<T, OutOfRange>`, separate from ArithmeticError.
docs/proposals/FOUNDATION.md:40:**Authority:** [no hidden traps](../decisions.md#decided-project-no-hidden-traps), [OD11](../decisions.md#od11), [OD99](../decisions.md#od99), [OD106](../decisions.md#od106), [OD114](../decisions.md#od114), TYPE-19, Core Q6.7. **Later:** operation syntax and admission instances; float rules at slice 26. The interface admits float instances but does not invent their unsettled details. No `isize` is added by implication: pointer differences use a mathematical signed offset in the model and an explicitly contracted representable result in source APIs.
docs/proposals/FOUNDATION.md:46:Layout laws: positive power-of-two alignment; size a multiple of aggregate alignment; occupied field bytes in bounds and non-overlapping; required field alignment in native/C modes; unique discriminants; and valid tag decoding. Only if question 5 adopts niches (unused encodings used for variants), require sound niche decoding and eligibility proofs. No niche machinery is required while niches are absent. Zero-size fields have empty byte ranges and may share offsets. Packed mode replaces natural field alignment with explicit unaligned-access premises; it must not falsely prove packed fields naturally aligned. Core uses the hybrid state in C4, with target/layout as a read-only parameter for sizeof/alignof and raw access. Safe typed operations are proved representation-independent where their premises permit it. The concrete algorithm below is one implementation proving these laws, not the premise of every earlier proof.
docs/proposals/FOUNDATION.md:48:**Rule:** native structs retain declaration order, each field rounded up to its alignment, aggregate alignment the maximum (1 for empty), and final size rounded up. Empty aggregates have size 0/alignment 1. Arrays have size `n × size(T)` and alignment `align(T)`, including `n = 0`. Fixed-width integer storage respects value width; alignment is target-defined. Q4.5 decides only StaticStr’s explicit pointer/byte-length FFI adapter, without an internal ABI promise. Q3.1–Q3.4 and Q4.6–Q4.7 decide view roots, loans and ranges, not their physical representation. Text length counts UTF-8 bytes. TYPE-3 Text owns immutable bytes, never aliases another value’s buffer, and is linear. Its ownership, allocator dependency and sufficient release context must survive representation; Q4.5’s FFI adapter does not fix Text’s internal size. No universal two-word Text layout is frozen. A common shared/mutable view representation is a proposal, not a decided rule. Defer its concrete layout to the view/layout slice, preserving the decided second-class semantics; no two-word view ABI is frozen. Address-space selection is explicit profile metadata, including program-memory text: no silent cast to data pointers. `Secret<T>` has T's layout if question 18 approves it; layout never implies constant time.
docs/proposals/FOUNDATION.md:54:**Authority:** [runtime/layout decision](../decisions.md#decided-project-runtime-2026-11-06), Core Q3.5–Q3.11, Q4.1–Q4.5; [OD72](../decisions.md#od72), [OD110](../decisions.md#od110), [OD111](../decisions.md#od111), [OD126](../decisions.md#od126), [OD147](../decisions.md#od147); FFI-4/7/8/10. Revisions are listed below. **Later:** syntax, additional target/ABI implementations, concrete allocation and FFI operations; not the layout laws or alignment premises.
docs/proposals/FOUNDATION.md:64:Raw writes update bytes/fragments and invalidate overlapping cached typed values. Every safe typed read of a materialized place itself applies partial Rep decoding to the current bytes, initialization, provenance and permissions; no user-level decode instruction is needed. Successful decoding supplies the valid value. Failure is unsafe stuckness, never a fabricated value or implicit repair; safe-admission preservation must exclude it under the stated trusted premises. Unmaterialized typed reads use structured values. Raw pointers, expose/from_exposed and sizeof/alignof therefore already have Core meanings. Layout is a read-only parameter. Memory CFG makes this representation and ABI explicit for every operation; it does not introduce raw memory for the first time. This keeps typed proofs abstract while allowing slice 24 to execute without replacing Core state.
docs/proposals/FOUNDATION.md:66:**Rule:** memory bytes are initialized octets or uninitialized. Allocation starts uninitialized unless its primitive contract initializes it. Track initialization per occupied field/element; moving out removes that slot's typed ownership/read permission. Reading a typed value requires its active representation bytes initialized and valid. Padding and inactive enum storage need not be initialized and cannot affect typed equality, typed control or typed observations. Initialized padding may be observed through authorized raw byte reads and then affect output. Representation simulations preserve such observed bytes, or explicitly relate the same allowed padding choices; they cannot erase a raw observation as irrelevant nondeterminism. Raw byte copying may transport uninitialized padding and provenance fragments without observing them; observing a byte requires initialization. `Rep` ignores padding but checks active tags, bool/char/text validity and pointer fragments. Prove encoding/decoding round-trip for valid typed values, with allowed padding choices, and that decoding yields only valid values.
docs/proposals/FOUNDATION.md:68:Block identity is never reused, even if an address is. Deallocation ends all access rights to the old block. Nonzero accesses need provenance, bounds, liveness, alignment (or justified unaligned mode), initialization for reads and write permission for writes. Q3.7’s zero-byte raw access needs only alignment and a non-null address. One-past pointers may be formed but not dereferenced. Address arithmetic cannot silently wrap into another allocation. Integers carry no provenance; `expose` records the block in the address space, and `from_exposed` uses Q3.9's in-bounds-first, then one-past, then no-provenance rule over live, nonempty eligible exposed blocks. Do not add a new arbitrary tie-break where the decided relation permits choices. Pointer equality follows the decided raw model, not equality of allocation identities alone.
docs/proposals/FOUNDATION.md:72:**Authority:** Core Q1.8–Q1.12, Q3.7–Q3.11; [memory decision](../decisions.md#decided-project-memory-2026-11-06). Hybrid state, initialization/padding and pointer-fragment completions require question 2; they do not revise Q3.7’s exception. **Later:** raw-pointer syntax (24), allocator algorithms (19–23), FFI operations (25). They instantiate this model rather than replace it.
docs/proposals/FOUNDATION.md:80:Fresh brand binders are nominal, including reset/loop generations; dependencies cannot escape their lifetime. Child allocators own backing and expose parent dependence. Grant narrowing consumes and intersects rights; acquisition preserves the parent as decided; close/give-up consumes its resource on all specified outcomes. Zero representation size never removes linear obligations. Permissions in `with(...)` are erased sets, distinct from runtime grants. `GrantTy` describes launch-grant types; File/Socket/Listener are acquired handles. Clock kind has separate identity/resolution/guarantee metadata. Entropy’s source is a named premise under the randomness decision; its exact grant shape and ending contract wait for slice 6 (question 14).
docs/proposals/FOUNDATION.md:82:Heap handles contain a pointer and sufficient stable release context, with any erasure requiring question 6 and a proof that release is statically known and needs no per-instance information. Allocator/grant representations are opaque per resource; grant state erases only if stream/device identity, rights and lifecycle are statically determined and all authority transitions are preserved. Prove erasure, rather than assuming one allocator or one capability means zero state.
docs/proposals/FOUNDATION.md:84:**Authority:** Core Q1.1–Q1.7, Q3.1–Q3.4, Q7.1–Q7.11; [OD15](../decisions.md#od15), [OD23](../decisions.md#od23), [authority decision](../decisions.md#decided-2026-11-02-authority), [randomness](../decisions.md#decided-2026-11-07-stack-randomness). **Later:** concrete loan/grant operations and source rules; no choice of a new stacked/tree borrow model replacing whole-root rules.
docs/proposals/FOUNDATION.md:90:**Rule:** model startup, capability requests and responses (including blocking), external/FFI effects, public output, logical terminal actions and shutdown. Bind external choices to a shared input/environment history. Error, partial-delivery and blocking behavior belongs to each operation contract; do not assume a hostile environment cooperates. Preserve output preceding a trap and externally visible final state. Normal exit and trap exit 134 remain different observations. Missing launch grants refuse startup with 125. Traps retain kind, typed site and the exact decided line `concrete: trap: <kind> at <path>:<line>:<col> (site <id>)`, project-relative forward-slash path, 1-based line and column; no per-build abbreviated variant. The kind inventory/rendering and byte-column/tab convention are questions 9–10. No abbreviated MCU format is introduced.
docs/proposals/FOUNDATION.md:96:**Authority:** Core Q6.7, Q7.5–Q7.10, Q8.4–Q8.6; [runtime](../decisions.md#decided-project-runtime-2026-11-06), [stack bounds](../decisions.md#decided-2026-11-07-stack-randomness), [concurrency last](../decisions.md#decided-2026-11-07-final-slices). The sequential preservation obligation is an interface law, not a chosen concurrent model. **Later:** grant-specific events and adapters; concurrency and atomic laws at slice 28, last. Public environment effects are not deterministic merely because input replay is deterministic.
docs/proposals/FOUNDATION.md:102:**Rule:** leakage records branch decisions, exact addresses, allocation sizes, loop counts and operands of variable-time operations, with public lengths and contracted external effects. A release record carries ordered (typed site, released value), with reason/path metadata for facts. The release-policy interface lists allowed functions/sites or forbids release, checks reachable releases through calls, and permits only explicit declared values. Its security obligation is delimited release: for equal public inputs, matched external choices and equal ordered release site/value traces, prove equal public observations (NI), and separately equal leakage traces (CT). Policy authorization alone does not establish either theorem or allow other secret effects. These are distinct decided claims, not an optional variable-time Secret tier.
docs/proposals/FOUNDATION.md:107:**Authority:** [information flow](../decisions.md#decided-project-infoflow-2026-11-06), [memory/accounting](../decisions.md#decided-project-memory-2026-11-06), Core Q9.10. **Later:** scalar secret rules/proofs at 7, arrays/wipes at 15 and heaps at 19. Binary constant time, hardware timing and actual wiping remain named backend premises until proved; speculative leakage is outside the decided observer model.
docs/proposals/FOUNDATION.md:117:**Authority:** Core Q6.6–Q6.7, Q9.9, [bounded execution](../decisions.md#decided-project-no-hidden-traps), [step bounds](../decisions.md#decided-2026-11-07-step-bounds). **Later:** concrete variants, loop/call cost instances and user proofs. Q9.9 is retained, not replaced by small-step semantics.
docs/proposals/FOUNDATION.md:127:**Authority:** Core Q1.5, Q6.3, Q9.1–Q9.2, Q9.6–Q9.7, Q9.10–Q9.13; [OD81](../decisions.md#od81), [OD125](../decisions.md#od125), [pipeline](../decisions.md#decided-project-pipeline-2026-11-06), [early evidence](../decisions.md#decided-2026-11-07-early-evidence). **Later:** primitive instances and syntax, module/invariant checking at 17. Q9.7's exhaustive inventory becomes exhaustive coverage of the selected registry, not a permanently closed universe.
docs/proposals/FOUNDATION.md:133:**Rule:** internal calls use an explicit target convention proved compatible with these contracts. At Memory CFG, lower the hybrid Core representation to explicit byte/provenance operations and ABI calls, proving its simulation against C4’s projection. BIR carries the complete resulting contract. Frame accounting binds to the final artifact, including spills, cleanup and launcher/runtime contributions, not merely source locals. Stack facts are separate from heap accounting. Foreign retention needs the decided `kept`/`Kept<H>` contract; FFI-safe types and no-enum restriction remain. Logical frame theorems do not add a source frame clause or snapshot construct.
docs/proposals/FOUNDATION.md:135:**Authority:** Core Q3.12, Q8.5–Q8.6, [pipeline](../decisions.md#decided-project-pipeline-2026-11-06), [contracts/P18](../decisions.md#decided-project-contracts), [stack bounds](../decisions.md#decided-2026-11-07-stack-randomness). **Later:** ABI implementations, extern syntax at 25, stronger program frame proofs. Neither the frame interface nor packed access waits until then.
docs/proposals/FOUNDATION.md:139:Retain the decided stages:
docs/proposals/FOUNDATION.md:143:BIR feeds the LLVM printer or the proved BIR→LCB adapter. **Memory CFG stays the single layout/ABI/provenance lowering boundary**; retract P2's folding proposal. Earlier semantics uses C4’s hybrid state and read-only layout interface; representation simulations relate it to this explicit lowering boundary.
docs/proposals/FOUNDATION.md:145:**Frontend:** retract P1's parse/print criterion. Define byte/token/grammar acceptance independently of the lexer/parser implementation, and source binding, typing and source-to-Core correspondence independently of resolve/elaboration. Prove lexer/parser correspondence to the independent grammar, resolution's binding preservation and elaboration's preservation of meaning, sequencing, cleanup, identities and source sites. Prove checker equivalence to independent admission judgments. Parse/print tests can supplement these proofs but cannot replace them. This retains Core Q8.3/Q9.13 and the decided direct frontend proofs.
docs/proposals/FOUNDATION.md:147:Keep direct proofs for sequencing, cleanup, representation and the BIR→LCB adapter. Preserve the decided option of **proved validators for Mono and SSA**, as well as SSA optimizations, register allocation and linking. A validator's acceptance theorem establishes the required correspondence; running a checker without that theorem is not proof. Mono re-admits its output.
docs/proposals/FOUNDATION.md:149:**Certificates and maximal behavior:** retain the [compiler-execution decision](../decisions.md#decided-project-long-term-2026-11-06): every authoritative compilation emits kernel-checked certificates binding the exact source bytes through independent judgments/validated edges to the exact emitted BIR, and binding the actual backend input to it. A digest establishes identity, not semantic correspondence. Native execution of proved Lean definitions is not itself proof; any gap retains explicit compiler/runtime/toolchain assumptions. After slice 0 use the decided per-edge certificates.
docs/proposals/FOUNDATION.md:155:**Qualification (P4):** run LLVM, LCB and the hybrid Core Lean evaluator on **every supported example and controlled test**, with the same input bytes, target/layout profile, grant configuration and compatible scripted external responses. Frontend certificates bind this Core program to the source; representation simulations relate its raw-byte/layout observations to both native paths. For each native run, check that its complete trace is an allowed observation of a Core execution under those inputs: this is a refinement check. The decided backend-order rule stays: LLVM and LCB full observations must equal each other and the expected outcome on every supported example, and any mismatch fails (question 17). Supported examples therefore do not observe allocation-dependent values such as exposed addresses or initialized padding. Native execution cannot be forced to follow the evaluator’s oracle, so the Core check finds and validates an admissible choice witness per native run, without normalizing away raw observations. Qualifying programs that legitimately observe such values is decided at slice 24. Failed witness search is inconclusive unless exclusion is proved. Compare complete observations: ordered requests/responses/output, terminal classification, trap kind/site/line and observable final external state. Use bounded scripted environments for effectful/blocking cases. Report unsupported or inconclusive runs explicitly; do not count `outOfFuel` as agreement or divergence. Compare leakage separately where the claimed backend evidence supports it. Never require arbitrary user programs to execute three times. Tests qualify implementations; the LCB path is **proved under named premises**, and LLVM remains unverified. Keep the two premise sets separate.
docs/proposals/FOUNDATION.md:171:Q9.11/OD144 already decide post-cleanup ensures timing. The SPARK decision already decides source struct invariants. Neither is a new rule. C4’s hybrid/representation completion is a proposed formalization choice, not authority to revise raw access. Exact trap constructors and byte columns are not inferred from Q6.7 or ArithmeticError. OD106/ARITH-10 already fix conversion errors; Q2.3 and Q3.8 already fix destruction orders. Questions 12 and 13 are removed as fully decided; later question numbers are retained for review tracking.
docs/proposals/FOUNDATION.md:175:Each recommendation is a proposal, not an implemented language decision. “Defer” preserves existing decided rules and leaves detailed instances to the named slice.
docs/proposals/FOUNDATION.md:177:<a id="q1"></a>**Q1. Foundation scope.** Recommend approving the interface/law boundary of C1–C10, with freeze only after implementation, kernel proofs, interaction tests, independent review and owner acceptance. Runner-up: freeze smaller integrated scopes incrementally. Neither approves the other numbered questions.
docs/proposals/FOUNDATION.md:178:<a id="q2"></a>**Q2. Hybrid state and representation completion.** Recommend C4’s structured-place/byte-block state, coherent projection, pointer fragments and explicit initialization/padding laws. Runner-up: use byte blocks for all Core storage. The hybrid keeps typed proofs small while raw operations remain executable; both must preserve Q3.7 and raw observations.
docs/proposals/FOUNDATION.md:181:<a id="q5"></a>**Q5. Niches.** Three options: (a) exactly one null-empty/non-null single-pointer-payload niche, with pointer-size, provenance, ownership and release-context proofs; (b) tagged layout now, future niches only by owner decision; (c) no niches permanently. Recommend **(b)**: the foundation needs representation laws, not a permanent optimization inventory. Runner-up **(a)** gives compact optional ownership but adds a special eligibility proof; multiword release contexts and nullable raw pointers are ineligible. Round-2 reviewers split: Codex and Kimi prefer (b); agy prefers (c). (c) is uniform but unnecessarily permanent.
docs/proposals/FOUNDATION.md:182:<a id="q6"></a>**Q6. Release-context erasure.** Recommend retaining sufficient stable context and erasing only with a release/identity/dependency preservation proof and no per-instance state. Runner-up: always store context. This separately revises Q1.7; it does not fix Text’s internal word count.
docs/proposals/FOUNDATION.md:184:<a id="q8"></a>**Q8. Native-image milestone.** Recommend keeping R-0505’s later closure and Q8.1’s conditional endpoint. Runner-up: require proved exact AArch64 linked-image validation by 0.1, explicitly revising Q8.1, early-evidence and proof-infrastructure milestones while retaining all undischarged premises. Qualification is question 17, not bundled here.
docs/proposals/FOUNDATION.md:185:<a id="q9"></a>**Q9. Trap inventory and spellings.** Recommend deferring concrete constructors/renderings until trap semantics needs them, before their first supported slice. Runner-up: approve `arithmetic(ArithmeticError) | bounds | invalidRange | abort`, rendered `overflow`, `division_by_zero`, `invalid_shift`, `bounds`, `invalid_range`, `abort`, with one trailing newline. ArithmeticError’s decided cases do not decide this wrapper or its strings. Preserve the runtime line format and exits either way. Codex and Kimi support deferral before first use; agy wants the inventory and spellings approved now to avoid churn.
docs/proposals/FOUNDATION.md:186:<a id="q10"></a>**Q10. Columns and tabs.** Recommend byte columns, tabs counting one byte, with conformance tests; runner-up: Unicode-scalar columns, tabs counting one scalar. The runtime decision fixes only 1-based columns, so neither convention is already decided.
docs/proposals/FOUNDATION.md:189:<a id="q15"></a>**Q15. Struct-invariant implementation.** Recommend deferring the source feature to slice 17, while writing the decided SPARK exposure definition in the spec before freeze and retaining Q9.11/OD144 post-cleanup ensures semantics. Runner-up: implement the source feature earlier if an interaction proof needs it. There is no new timing choice to approve.
docs/proposals/FOUNDATION.md:191:<a id="q17"></a>**Q17. Differential qualification.** Recommend keeping the decided [backend-order](../decisions.md#decided-project-pipeline-2026-11-06) rule unchanged: on every supported example, LLVM and LCB full observations must equal each other and the expected outcome, and any mismatch fails. Add, on top of it, the check that each native trace is an allowed observation of the Core semantics. Supported examples must not observe allocation-dependent values (exposed addresses, initialized padding), so equality stays meaningful. How to qualify programs that legitimately observe such values (raw pointers, slice 24) is an open question for that slice, not relaxed now. Runner-up: relax cross-backend equality to per-backend refinement for such programs now, which revises the backend-order row. Kimi found draft 3 relaxed that decided rule without registering it; this fixes that.
docs/proposals/FOUNDATION.md:193:<a id="q18"></a>**Q18. Secret<T> layout.** Recommend that `Secret<T>` has exactly the layout of T (zero-cost wrapper). Layout does not establish constant time; the information-flow rules do. The [information-flow decision](../decisions.md#decided-project-infoflow-2026-11-06) covers typing, Copy, wiping and leakage, not representation, so this is a new rule (Kimi, round 3). Runner-up: an opaque representation fixed per slice. All three reviewers chose identical layout in the earlier step-3 round.
docs/proposals/SPEC_UNIFICATION.md:14:- `origin/main` ab537833d7 and `origin/wip/decisions-followup` 4e75883d91: `docs/decisions.md` and `ROADMAP.md`. The three decided proposals are in [`docs/archive/crucial-2026-11-06/`](../archive/crucial-2026-11-06/).
docs/proposals/SPEC_UNIFICATION.md:43:There are also four instrumentation domains with one erasure proof between them.
